### Challenges

It took quite a long time to develop the five layers. If I increased the robot speed, the plastic strips could slip and was not  placed in the exact positions. Therefore, I had to position each strip carefully, one by one, to maintain the shape and alignment of the structure.


I stopped the development at the fifth layer because of the limited time available. However, more layers can be added similaly with stable.

Another challenge was that I had not marked the centre of each strip. When I defined the pickup coordinates, the pickup position was not properly aligned with the centre of the strip. As a result, some strips were lightly shifted from their intended positions when building the layers. 

To improve this, I should mark the centre of each strip and define the exact storage positions. This would help the robot pick up the strips consistently and place them more accurately.


There were also many coordinates to enter into DobotStudio. I tried to export the coordinates, but I could not succeed. Therefore, I had to enter the coordinates manually, one by one. This took additional time. In the future, I should find a more efficient way to transfer the coordinates to the robot.

### Prictical situations 

This project demonstrates how a robot can pick, rotate, and place objects at different angles to build a structure. The same concept can be adapted to different practical applications by changing the robot program, the object positions, and the gripper.

1. Packing
2. Supermarket goods arrangement
3. Wood Stacking
4. Assembly operation

In this project, I used a vacuum suction gripper to pick up the strips. In practical applications, different grippers can be selected depending on the shape, size, weight, and material of the objects.

Finally, I could understand the importance of coordinates defining, correct alignment, robot speed, careful placementsin robot operation system.


## 2026-10-17  Simulation with Robodk

### Pick and Place Location Setup and Robot Programming

I had already completed the pick (stacking) location arrangement. Then, I arranged the place locations using Kapla wood blocks according to my design. First, I identified the known coordinates and dimensions of a Kapla wood block. Then, I manually calculated the other placement coordinates based on my design.

After that, I defined the pick and place locations, as well as the approach locations for picking and placing the wooden blocks.

First, I learned about the coordinate systems, including the suction cup coordinates relative to the robot flange, the reference frame relative to the robot base, and the tool frame relative to the reference frame. I also learned about the positive and negative directions of the X, Y, and Z axes. This knowledge helped me to calculate and define the coordinates more easily.


Challenges : When I entered the manually calculated coordinates into the robot and tried to move the robot arm to the pick and place locations, I found that some locations were outside the robot's reachable range. Therefore, I checked the robot's working range again and adjusted the pick and place locations to make sure the robot could reach them.


Then, I created the robot programme and named it FantasticFactory. I defined all the movements as linear movements to provide controlled and predictable paths between the pick and place locations.

I also carefully checked the rotation angles of the wooden blocks because each block needed to be placed at a different angle to create my planned structure.

Finally, I created the complete movement sequence and ran the programme. The programme ran successfully, and the robot completed the movements without any collisions.


